Prefix(rdfs:=<http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#>)


Ontology(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/flopo/flopo-bearer-axioms.owl>
Import(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/flopo/releases/2026-11-24/flopo.owl>)
<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/flopo/releases/2026-11-24/flopo-bearer-axioms.ofn>
Annotation(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/license> <https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/>)
Annotation(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/title> "FLOPO bearer axioms: from a phenotype to the plant that has it (OWL 2 EL bridge)"@en)
Annotation(rdfs:comment "Generated by tools/build_flopo_phene_bearers.py from flopo.owl of the same version. A FLOPO phenotype class P is defined as 'flora phenotype' and 'phenotype of' some Y, where Y is a property of the plant. 'phenotype of' (RO:0002201) is functional and the inverse of 'has phenotype' (RO:0002200), so a plant that has P has the property Y. Reasoners for OWL 2 EL, such as ELK, do not read functional and inverse properties. This file states the consequence once per class: 'has phenotype' some P SubClassOf Y. Every axiom here is entailed by flopo.owl; nothing is added for an OWL 2 DL reasoner. Use it with ELK and data of the form Taxon SubClassOf 'has phenotype' some P: the taxon is classified under the defining properties of its phenotypes, and a taxon whose phenotypes cannot hold of one plant is unsatisfiable. The axioms are in OWL 2 EL wherever the defining property is. Documentation: docs/phene-consistency.md in the FLOPO repository."@en)
Annotation(owl:versionInfo "2026-11-24")

